which is $\ll 1$ since $J \leqslant \log K$, ${\log H_0 \gg K^{1-\varepsilon_1}}$ and $T \gg K^{1/3}$.

\label{lem:prohibitedanalysis2}
Let $2<\ell\leqslant L$ and let $\boldsymbol{d} = (d_1, \ldots, d_{\ell})$ be a primitive prohibited sequence. 

Let $\Gamma$ be the set of all constraints $C$ satisfied by $\boldsymbol{d}$, that are of the form $C = C_{I,i_0,j_0,0}(\boldsymbol{d})$ for some discrete interval $I\subset \sinterval{\ell}$ and some $(i_0,j_0)\in \sinterval{\ell} \!\times\! \sinterval{J}$.

For every prime $p\mid \fprod{\boldsymbol{d}}$, 
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \label{item:prohibitedanalysis1} either there is a constraint $C \in \Gamma$ in which $p$ is involved,
	\item \label{item:prohibitedanalysis2} or there is another prime $q$ involved in a constraint of $\Gamma$, such that $q \mid d_{i'}$ for some $i'\in \sinterval{\ell}$ and
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{\substack{1\leqslant i <i'\\ p\mid d_i}} d_i \not\equiv 0 \pmod q.
	\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}

For $p\in \mathcal{P}$, let 
\begin{equation*}
	I(p) := \{i\in \sinterval{{\ell}}: p\mid d_i\},
\end{equation*}
it is a discrete interval by definition of a prohibited sequence (\cref{def:prohibitedsequence}). 

By \cref{def:prohibitedsequence}, there are some $1<\ell_0<\ell$ and $j_0\in \sinterval{J}$ such that $\pcomp{1}{j_0}\nmid d_{\ell}$ and
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prohibitedanalysiscstrt}
	\pcomp{1}{j_0} \mid \sum_{\ell_0\leqslant i\leqslant \ell} d_i.
\end{equation}
In particular, the constraint $C_1 := C_{\interval{\ell_0}{\ell},1,j_0,0}(\boldsymbol{d})$ is satisfied by $\boldsymbol{d}$, so $C_1 \in \Gamma$.

Among all the primes dividing $d_{\ell}$, choose some prime $p_{1}$ such that $I(p_{1})$ is minimal for inclusion. We claim that $p_{1}$ is \cref{item:const3}-involved in $C_1$. Clearly $\pcomp{1}{j_0}\neq p_{1}$ since $\pcomp{1}{j_0}\nmid d_{\ell}$. Moreover, it is easy to see that $d_i = d_{\ell}$ for all $i\in I(p_{1})$, using the fact that $I(p_{1})$ is minimal for inclusion and the first two assumptions of \cref{def:prohibitedsequence}, as in the proof of \cref{lem:seprepstep0}. Hence,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prohibitedanalysis0}
	\sum_{\substack{i\in \interval{\ell_0}{\ell}\\ p_{1}\mid d_i}}d_i = \abs{I(p_{1})\cap \interval{\ell_0}{\ell}} d_{\ell}.
\end{equation}
Since $\abs{I(p_{1})\cap \interval{\ell_0}{\ell}}\leqslant L < H_0 < \pcomp{1}{j_0}$ and $\pcomp{1}{j_0}\nmid d_{\ell}$, the expression \cref{eq:prohibitedanalysis0} is not divisible by ${\pcomp{1}{j_0}}$, which means that $p_{1}$ is \cref{item:const3}-involved in $C_1$ as claimed.

We are now ready to start the proof of \cref{lem:prohibitedanalysis2} in earnest. Let $p\mid \fprod{\boldsymbol{d}}$ be a prime. 

If $I(p) \subset I(p_{1})$, then $I(p) = I(p_{1})$ by minimality of $I(p_{1})$. Repeating the previous paragraph with $p$ in place of $p_{1}$, we conclude that $p$ is involved in $C_1$, so we are in case~\cref{item:prohibitedanalysis1}. We henceforth assume that $I(p) \setminus I(p_{1})$ is non-empty. Note that $I(p) \setminus I(p_{1})$ is discrete interval; we denote it by $\interval{a_1}{a_2}$.

Assume that $\sum_{\substack{i\in \sinterval{\ell},\, p\mid d_i}} d_i \equiv 0 \pmod{p_{1}}$, as otherwise we are in case \cref{item:prohibitedanalysis2} with $q = p_{1}$. This can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prohibitedanalysis}
	p_{1} \ \Big\vert\,  \sum_{i\in \interval{a_1}{a_2}} d_i.
\end{equation} 
However, this implies that $\big(d_{\ell}, d_{\ell-1}, \ldots, d_{a_1+1}, d_{a_1}\big)$ is a prohibited sequence. Since $\boldsymbol{d}$ is a primitive prohibited sequence, this is only possible if $a_1=1$. Hence, $I(p) \setminus I(p_{1}) = \interval{1}{a_2}$. Note that $p_{1} \nmid d_1$ and thus $a_2\geqslant 2$ by \cref{eq:prohibitedanalysis}.

We will now exhibit another prime $p_2$ for which the case \cref{item:prohibitedanalysis2} of the lemma holds with $q = p_2$.

Let $p_2$ be the prime $\pcomp{2}{j_0}$. Note that $p_2 \nmid d_1$, or else we would have $p_2 = \pcomp{1}{j_0}$, and thus ${p_2 \mid \sum_{\ell_0\leqslant i\leqslant \ell} d_i}$ by \cref{eq:prohibitedanalysiscstrt}. This would imply that $(d_{2}, d_{3}, \ldots, d_{\ell})$ is a prohibited sequence, which is impossible since $\boldsymbol{d}$ is primitive. 

Next, observe that \cref{eq:prohibitedanalysis} is exactly saying that $\boldsymbol{d}$ satisfies the constraint ${C_2 := C_{\interval{1}{a_2},\ell,j_1,0}(\boldsymbol{d})}$, where $j_1$ is the unique integer such that $p_{1} = \pcomp{\ell}{j_1}$. Let us show that $p_2$ is \cref{item:const3}-involved in $C_2$. Recall that $\interval{1}{a_2} = I(p) \setminus I(p_{1}) \supset \{1,2\}$, so $p_{1} \nmid d_2$ and hence $p_2 \neq p_{1}$. Suppose for contradiction that $p_2$ is not \cref{item:const3}-involved in $C_2$. Then, we would have
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:uxtfgt}
	p_{1} \ \Big\vert\, \sum_{\substack{i\in \interval{1}{a_2}\\ p_2 \mid d_i}} d_i = \sum_{\substack{i\in \interval{2}{a_2}\\ p_2 \mid d_i}} d_i,
\end{equation}
using that $p_2 \nmid d_1$. Note that $\{i\in \interval{2}{a_2}: p_2 \mid d_i\}$ is a discrete interval containing $2$ and not containing $\ell$. Thus, \cref{eq:uxtfgt} implies that $\big(d_{\ell}, d_{\ell-1}, \ldots, d_{2}\big)$ is a prohibited sequence, contradicting that $\boldsymbol{d}$ is primitive. Hence, $p_2$ is \cref{item:const3}-involved in $C_2$.

To summarise, we have shown that the prime $p_2$ is involved in $C_2\in \Gamma$. Since $p_2 \mid d_2$, we can easily check that case \cref{item:prohibitedanalysis2} applies with $q=p_2$ and $i' = 2$: $p_2 \mid d_2$ and
\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{\substack{1\leqslant i<2\\ p\mid d_i}} d_i = d_1 \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p_2}.
\end{equation*}
This concludes the proof.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:uxtfgt}
	p_{1} \ \Big\vert\, \sum_{\substack{i\in \interval{1}{a_2}\\ p_2 \mid d_i}} d_i = \sum_{\substack{i\in \interval{2}{a_2}\\ p_2 \mid d_i}} d_i,
\end{equation}

Recall that $\mathbb{Z} \setminus Y_{\!L}$ is the union of all prohibited progressions $P\in \mathcal{Y}$. By the union bound, we have
\begin{equation*}
	\abs{I_N \setminus Y_{\!L}} \leqslant \sum_{P\in \mathcal{Y}} \abs{I_N \cap P} \ll N \sum_{P\in \mathcal{Y}} \frac{1}{q_{P}}.
\end{equation*}
For any $P\in \mathcal{Y}$, there is a primitive prohibited sequence $\boldsymbol{d}$ of length $\leqslant L$ such that $P$ is the prohibited progression associated to $\boldsymbol{d}$. By \cref{lem:prohibitedanalysis2}, there is a constraint $C$ satisfied by $\boldsymbol{d}$ which involves at least one prime. This constraint alone can be viewed as a triangular system of complexity $(1; 1, 0)$. We apply \cref{lem:constraints} with $\mathbf{T} = \mathbf{T}_{\ell}$ being the set of ${\boldsymbol{d}}\in (\pm \mathcal{D})^{\ell}$ satisfying a triangular system of complexity $(1; 1, 0)$, for $2<\ell\leqslant L$. This gives 
\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{P\in \mathcal{Y}} \frac{1}{q_{P}} \leqslant \sum_{2<\ell\leqslant L}\, \sum_{\boldsymbol{d}\in \mathbf{T}_{\ell}}\, \prod_{p\mid \fprod{\boldsymbol{d}}} \frac{1}{p} \ll L K^{11L J} H_0^{-1/2}.
\end{equation*}
This is $\ll H_0^{-1/3}$ since $J \leqslant \log K$, ${\log H_0 \gg K^{1-\varepsilon_1}}$ and $L \ll K^{1-10\varepsilon_1}$.

\label{def:rank}
Let $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{L}$, $\mathcal{U}$ be sets such that $\sinterval{K}\times\sinterval{J} = \mathcal{S}\sqcup\mathcal{L}\sqcup\mathcal{U}$ and let $\boldsymbol{d} \in \mathbf{D}^{\mathcal{S}}_{\!K}$. We define $A_{\boldsymbol{d}} \subset \mathbb{Z}$ to be the arithmetic progression
\begin{equation*}
	A_{\boldsymbol{d}} := \{n\in \mathbb{Z} : \forall (i,j)\in \mathcal{L}, \ \pcomp{i}{j} \mid n+b_i\}.
\end{equation*}
Let $R \in (\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$. If $R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = \emptyset$, we set $\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R) := +\infty$. Otherwise, we define $\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R)$ to be the largest integer $T \geqslant 0$ for which there exist progressions $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{T}\in \mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b}$ containing $R$ such that, for each $t\in \sinterval{T}$, the modulus $q_{Q_t}$ does not divide $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}\prod_{s\in \sinterval{T}\setminus \{t\}}q_{Q_s}$.

\label{lem:rankintersection}
Let $R \in (\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$ be such that $\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R) = T < +\infty$. Then, there are progressions ${Q_1, \ldots, Q_{T}\in \mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b}}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
	\emptyset \neq R \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = \bigcap_{t\in\sinterval{T}} Q_t \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}.
\end{equation*}

By definition of $(\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$, we may write $R = \bigcap_{i\in I} Q_i$ for some finite set $I$ and some $Q_i\in \mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b}$. Let~$I_0$ be a minimal subset of $I$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:writeintersection}
	R \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = \bigcap_{i\in I_0} Q_i \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}.
\end{equation}
Note that the modulus of a non-empty intersection of arithmetic progressions is the least common multiple of the moduli of these progressions.  
There is no $i_0\in I_0$ such that $q_{Q_{i_0}}$ divides $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}\prod_{i\in I_0\setminus \{i_0\}}q_{Q_i}$, for otherwise $\bigcap_{i\in I_0\setminus \{i_0\}} Q_i \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$ and $\bigcap_{i\in I_0} Q_i \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$ would have the same modulus, so these progressions would be equal, contradicting the minimality of $I_0$. This shows that $\abs{I_0} \leqslant \mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R)$. Thus, \cref{eq:writeintersection} means that we have been able to write $R \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$ as an intersection of at most $\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R)$ progressions $Q_i \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$. Repeating some $Q_i$ if necessary, we can make it an intersection of exactly $\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R)$ sets.

Let $\boldsymbol{d}\in \mathbf{D}^{\mathcal{S}}_{\!K}$ and let $R\in (\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$ be a progression with ${\mathrm{rank}_{\boldsymbol{d}}(R) = T < +\infty}$. By \cref{lem:rankintersection}, there are ${Q_1, \ldots, Q_{T}\in \mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b}}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
	\emptyset \neq R \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = \bigcap_{t\in\sinterval{T}} Q_t \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}.
\end{equation*}
Property \cref{item:rank2} follows, since
\begin{equation*}
	\omega(q_{R}) \leqslant \omega(q_{R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant \sum_{t\in\sinterval{T}} \omega(q_{Q_t}) + \omega(q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant T L J + K J.
\end{equation*}
For property \cref{item:rank2bis}, write $\omega_{\mathcal{S}}(n) := \sum_{p\mid \fprod{\boldsymbol{d}; \mathcal{S}}} \ind{p\mid n}$. We similarly obtain
\begin{equation*}
	\omega_{\mathcal{S}}(q_{R}) \leqslant \omega_{\mathcal{S}}(q_{R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant \sum_{t\in\sinterval{T}} \omega_{\mathcal{S}}(q_{Q_t}) + \omega_{\mathcal{S}}(q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant T L J + 0
\end{equation*}
as $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}$ is only divisible by the primes $p\mid \fprod{\boldsymbol{d}; \mathcal{L}}$.

\begin{equation*}
	\omega(q_{R}) \leqslant \omega(q_{R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant \sum_{t\in\sinterval{T}} \omega(q_{Q_t}) + \omega(q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}) \leqslant T L J + K J.
\end{equation*}

\label{lem:mainboundexcludedprimes}
Let $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{L}$, $\mathcal{U}$ be sets such that $\sinterval{K}\times\sinterval{J} = \mathcal{S}\sqcup\mathcal{L}\sqcup\mathcal{U}$ and let $\boldsymbol{d}\in \mathbf{D}^{\mathcal{S}}_{\!K}$. Let $T = \lceil K^{5\varepsilon_1} \rceil$. Let $\mathcal{Y}_{T}$ be a set whose elements are progressions $R\in (\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$ for which there exist $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{T}\in \mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:expressionR1}
	\emptyset \neq R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = \bigcap_{t\in \sinterval{T}} Q_t \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}.
\end{equation}
Let $\mathcal{P}' \subset \mathcal{P}$ be a set of size $\leqslant 2KJ$ containing the prime divisors of $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}$. Then
\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}} \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}} \frac{1}{p} \ll e^{O(KJ)}.
\end{equation*}

Let $\mathcal{X}$ be the set of all $R_1\in (\mathcal{Y}-\boldsymbol{b})^{\cap}$ of the form $R_1 = R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$ for some $R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}$. Since the prime factors of $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}$ are in $\mathcal{P}'$, we may rewrite
\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{\substack{R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}}} \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}' }}\frac{1}{p} = \sum_{R_1\in \mathcal{X}}  \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R_1}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}}\frac{1}{p} \sum_{\substack{R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}\\ R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = R_1}} 1.
\end{equation*}
To bound the inner sum, we use the following fact: for any arithmetic progression $R_1$ and any $d\mid q_{R_1}$, there is a unique arithmetic progression $R\supset R_1$ with $q_{R_1}/q_{R} = d$, and moreover all progressions $R\supset R_1$ are obtained in this way. Therefore, the inner sum is bounded by the number of divisors of $q_{R_1}$. For every $R_1\in \mathcal{X}$, we have ${\omega(q_{R_1}) \ll KJ}$. This follows from \cref{eq:expressionR1} as in the proof of part \cref{item:rank2} of \cref{lem:existencerank}. Therefore, $q_{R_1}$ has $e^{O(KJ)}$ divisors, and hence the inner sum is $e^{O(KJ)}$.

It remains to show that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:partrank4}
	\sum_{R_1\in \mathcal{X}}  \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R_1}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}}\frac{1}{p} \ll e^{O(KJ)}.
\end{equation}
This is a simple counting problem, similar to \cref{lem:trivialbound} or \cref{prop:predictablevector}. However, the notation is much heavier in this case.

Let $R_1\in \mathcal{X}$. By definition of $\mathcal{Y}_{T}$ and $\mathcal{X}$, we can write 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:reprR1}
	R_1 = \bigcap_{t\in \sinterval{T}} \big(Q_t - b_{k_t}\big) \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}
\end{equation}
for some $Q_t\in \mathcal{Y}$ and some $k_t\in \sinterval{K}$. For $t\in \sinterval{T}$, let $\boldsymbol{d}^{(t)}$ be a primitive prohibited sequence having $Q_t$ as its associated prohibited progression. Let $\ell_t$ be the length of $\boldsymbol{d}^{(t)}$ and let $\sigma_t\in \{\pm1\}^{\ell_t}$ be the sequence of signs of the coordinates of $\boldsymbol{d}^{(t)}$. As usual, for $(i,j)\in \sinterval{\ell_t} \!\times \!\sinterval{J}$ we write $d^{(t)}_{ij}$ for the unique prime in $\mathcal{P}_j$ dividing $d^{(t)}_i$. Let $\sim$ be the equivalence relation on $\bigsqcup_{t\in \sinterval{T}} \big(\{t\}\!\times \!\sinterval{\ell_t} \!\times \!\sinterval{J}\big)$ defined by 
\begin{equation*}
	(t_1,i_1,j_1) \sim (t_2,i_2,j_2) \quad \iff \quad d^{(t_1)}_{i_1j_1} = d^{(t_2)}_{i_2j_2}.
\end{equation*}
If $\alpha$ is an equivalence class for $\sim$, we write $p_{\alpha}$ for the prime $d^{(t)}_{ij}$, where $(t,i,j)$ is any element of~$\alpha$. This definition does not depend on the choice of representative, by definition of $\sim$. Let $E$ be the set of all equivalence classes $\alpha$ for $\sim$ such that $p_{\alpha} \in \mathcal{P}'$. Let $\phi : E \to\mathcal{P}'$ be the map defined by $\phi(\alpha) = p_{\alpha}$. We call the tuple $((k_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\ell_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\sigma_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, \sim, E, \phi)$ a \emph{template} for $R_1$. Thus, to every progression $R_1\in \mathcal{X}$ we may associate a template (note that there may not be a canonical choice for the template associated to $R_1$, as it depends on the choice of a representation of $R_1$ as in~\cref{eq:reprR1}).

Let $\Theta$ be the set of all tuples $((k_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\ell_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\sigma_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, \sim, E, \phi)$ which are a template of some element $R_1 \in \mathcal{X}$. Fix some $\theta = ((k_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\ell_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, (\sigma_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}, \sim, E, \phi) \in \Theta$. Let $\mathcal{X}_\theta$ be the set of all $R_1\in \mathcal{X}$ for which $\theta$ is a template. Suppose that $\mathcal{X}_\theta$ is non-empty. Any $R_1\in \mathcal{X}_\theta$ is uniquely determined by the sequence of primes $(p_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in E'}$, where $E'$ is the set of all equivalence classes of $\sim$ not in $E$. Thus
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:partrank4bis}
	\sum_{R_1\in \mathcal{X}_\theta}  \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R_1}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}}\frac{1}{p} \leqslant \sum_{(p_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in E'}} \prod_{\alpha\in E'} \frac{1}{p_{\alpha}} \leqslant V^{\abs{E'}} \leqslant V^{T L J} \leqslant e^{O(KJ)},
\end{equation}
where we used that $T \ll K^{5\varepsilon_1}$ and $L<K^{1-10\varepsilon_1}$ in the last inequality. 

We proceed to sum \cref{eq:partrank4bis} over all choices of $\theta\in \Theta$. We will be done provided that the number of possible templates is $e^{O(KJ)}$. The number of choices for $(k_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}$, $(\ell_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}$ and $(\sigma_t)_{t\in \sinterval{T}}$ is at most $K^{T}$, $L^{T}$ and $(2^{L})^{T}$ respectively. Since $\sim$ is an equivalence relation on a set of size $\leqslant T L J$, there are $\leqslant (T L J)^{T L J}$ choices for $\sim$. There are $\leqslant 2^{T L J}$ choices for $E$. Finally, $\phi$ is a map from a set of size $\leqslant T L J$ to a set of size $\leqslant 2 KJ$, so there are $\leqslant (2KJ)^{T L J}$ possibilities for $\phi$. In summary, the number of templates is
\begin{equation*}
 \leqslant K^{T} \cdot L^{T} \cdot (2^{L})^{T} \cdot (T L J)^{T L J} \cdot 2^{T L J} \cdot (2KJ)^{T L J} = e^{O(KJ)}.
\end{equation*}
This concludes the proof of \cref{lem:mainboundexcludedprimes}.

\begin{equation*}
	\sum_{\substack{R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}}} \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}' }}\frac{1}{p} = \sum_{R_1\in \mathcal{X}}  \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R_1}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}}\frac{1}{p} \sum_{\substack{R\in \mathcal{Y}_{T}\\ R\cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} = R_1}} 1.
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:partrank4bis}
	\sum_{R_1\in \mathcal{X}_\theta}  \prod_{\substack{p\mid q_{R_1}\\ p\not\in \mathcal{P}'}}\frac{1}{p} \leqslant \sum_{(p_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in E'}} \prod_{\alpha\in E'} \frac{1}{p_{\alpha}} \leqslant V^{\abs{E'}} \leqslant V^{T L J} \leqslant e^{O(KJ)},
\end{equation}

\begin{equation*}
 \leqslant K^{T} \cdot L^{T} \cdot (2^{L})^{T} \cdot (T L J)^{T L J} \cdot 2^{T L J} \cdot (2KJ)^{T L J} = e^{O(KJ)}.
\end{equation*}

\label{lem:remainderterm}
Let $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{L}$, $\mathcal{U}$ be sets such that $\sinterval{K}\times\sinterval{J} = \mathcal{S}\sqcup\mathcal{L}\sqcup\mathcal{U}$ and $|\mathcal{U}| \leqslant K^{2\varepsilon_1}$, and let $\boldsymbol{d}\in \mathbf{D}^{\mathcal{S},\mathcal{L}}_{\!K}$. Let $T \geqslant 8 K^{2\varepsilon_1}$ and let $k_1\leqslant k_2\leqslant \cdots \leqslant k_{T}$ be elements of $\sinterval{K}$. Let $\boldsymbol{d}^{(1)}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{d}^{(T)}$ be primitive prohibited sequences, and let $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{T}\in \mathcal{Y}$ be the associated prohibited progressions. Suppose that, for each $t\in \sinterval{T}$, the modulus $q_{Q_t}$ does not divide $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}\prod_{s\in \sinterval{T}\setminus \{t\}}q_{Q_s}$, and that
\begin{equation*}
	\bigcap_{t\in \sinterval{T}} \big(Q_t - b_{k_t}\big) \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}} \neq \emptyset.
\end{equation*}
Let $\boldsymbol{v}$ be the sequence obtained by concatenation of $\boldsymbol{d}, \boldsymbol{d}^{(1)}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{d}^{(T)}, -\boldsymbol{d}^{(1)}, \ldots, -\boldsymbol{d}^{(T)}$. Then, $\boldsymbol{v}$ satisfies a triangular system of complexity $(\lfloor \tfrac{1}{16}T\rfloor; 3, 0)$.

Fix some $n\in \bigcap_{t=1}^{T} \big(Q_t - b_{k_t}\big) \cap A_{\boldsymbol{d}}$. 

For every $t\in \sinterval{T}$, let $\Gamma_t$ be the set of all constraints satisfied by $\boldsymbol{d}^{(t)}$ of the form $C_{I,i_0,j_0,0}(\boldsymbol{d})$ for some discrete interval $I\subset \sinterval{\nellp{t}}$ and some $(i_0,j_0)\in \sinterval{\nellp{t}} \!\times\! \sinterval{J}$, where $\nellp{t}$ is the length of $\boldsymbol{d}^{(t)}$.

Suppose first that there is a set $\mathcal{I}\subset \sinterval{T}$ of size $\geqslant \tfrac{1}{16} T$ such that, for every $t\in \mathcal{I}$, there is a constraint $C_t\in \Gamma_t$ and a prime $p_t$ which is involved in $C_t$ and does not divide $\prod_{s<t}q_{Q_s}$. Then, clearly, $p_t$ is absent from $C_s$, for every $s\in \mathcal{I}$ with $s<t$, which means that the constraints $(C_t)_{t\in \mathcal{I}}$ form a triangular system of complexity $(\lfloor \tfrac{1}{16} T\rfloor; 1, 0)$. The same conclusion holds if there is a set $\mathcal{I}\subset \sinterval{T}$ of size $\geqslant \tfrac{1}{16} T$ such that, for every $t\in \mathcal{I}$, there is a constraint $C_t\in \Gamma_t$ and a prime $p_t$ which is involved in $C_t$ and does not divide $\prod_{s>t}q_{Q_s}$. We may thus assume that, for $\geqslant \tfrac{7}{8} T$ values of $t\in \sinterval{T}$, every prime involved in some constraint of $\Gamma_t$ divides both $\prod_{s<t}q_{Q_s}$ and $\prod_{s>t}q_{Q_s}$.

For every $t\in \sinterval{T}$, fix a prime $p_t$ dividing $q_{Q_t}$ but not dividing $q_{A_{\boldsymbol{d}}}\prod_{s\in \sinterval{T}\setminus \{t\}}q_{Q_s}$. This is possible by the assumption in the statement. We apply \cref{lem:prohibitedanalysis2} with this prime $p_t$. Note that the first case of \cref{lem:prohibitedanalysis2} can only occur for $<\tfrac{1}{8}T$ values of $t\in \sinterval{T}$ by definition of $p_t$ and the previous paragraph. Let $\mathcal{I}_1$ be the set of $t\in \sinterval{T}$ such that the second case holds, i.e.~for $t\in \mathcal{I}_1$ there is a prime $q_t$ involved in a constraint $C_t\in \Gamma_t$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:propertyofpt}
	\sum_{\substack{1\leqslant i<i_t\\ p_t\mid d^{(t)}_i}} d^{(t)}_i \not\equiv 0 \pmod{q_t},
\end{equation}
where $i_t\in \sinterval{\nellp{t}}$ is such that $q_t\mid d^{(t)}_{i_t}$. Thus $\abs{\mathcal{I}_1}\geqslant \tfrac78 T$. 

By our earlier observation, there is a subset $\mathcal{I}_2\subset \mathcal{I}_1$ of size $\geqslant \tfrac12 T$ such that, for all $t\in \mathcal{I}_2$, there are $1\leqslant s_1(t)<t<s_2(t)\leqslant T$ with $q_t\mid q_{Q_{s_1(t)}}$ and $q_t\mid q_{Q_{s_2(t)}}$.

